// ===========================================================================
//  Logistics Delay-Risk Prediction & Carbon Cost Dashboard
//  DAX measures — plain-text reference for Lojistik_Erken_Uyari_Paneli.pbix
// ===========================================================================
//
//  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
//  --------------------
//  A .pbix is a binary. Its measures cannot be diffed, code-reviewed, or read
//  by anyone without Power BI Desktop installed. This file mirrors them as text
//  so the semantic model is reviewable alongside the Python code.
//
//  PROVENANCE AND VERIFICATION STATUS  ***READ THIS***
//  ---------------------------------------------------
//  Everything below is verified. Nothing in this file is a reconstruction.
//
//  Measure NAMES and field bindings were extracted programmatically from the
//  .pbix report definition (Report/definition/pages/**/visual.json), which is
//  plain JSON in the PBIR format. The JSON was walked structurally — as a tree,
//  not with a regex over a flattened blob, because a non-greedy regex mis-pairs
//  a field name with the aggregation code that follows it.
//
//  Measure EXPRESSIONS were read out of the live model with DAX Studio,
//  connected to the running Power BI Desktop instance:
//
//      SELECT [Name], [Expression] FROM $SYSTEM.TMSCHEMA_MEASURES
//
//  The query returned EXACTLY FOUR ROWS — the four measures below. The model
//  contains no other measure: none hidden, none unused, none sitting on no
//  visual. That is a complete enumeration from the semantic model itself, not
//  an inference from the report definition, and it closes the one gap the
//  report definition could never close.
//
//  (The `DataModel` stream itself stays unreadable by direct parsing — it is
//  compressed VertiPaq, and neither the measure names nor "DIVIDE" occur in it
//  as ASCII or UTF-16LE. DAX Studio queries the loaded model, not the file.)
//
//  Extracted from : powerbi/Lojistik_Erken_Uyari_Paneli.pbix
//  SHA-256        : 7054068B877B21B7FC1E39E202FDE6833BF272535D3C64B8B3D6099CAB97EB81
//  Size           : 158,916 bytes
//  Pages          : "Executive Summary", "Early Warning Panel"
//  Fact table     : Fact_Shipments_with_ML
//  Dimensions     : Dim_Vendor, Dim_Route, Dim_Date
//
//  This file supersedes two earlier versions: one written from documentation
//  alone, and one written from a superseded .pbix that did not match the
//  screenshots. Both are gone; the divergence they recorded is resolved.
// ===========================================================================


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SECTION 1 — Every measure in the model  [COMPLETE — 4 of 4]
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TMSCHEMA_MEASURES returned four rows and these are those four. Every one of
// them is also bound to at least one visual, so the model carries no dead
// measures.


// Used on: "Executive Summary" — KPI card (reads 1,23K), and the column chart
//          "Total CO2 Tons by Vehicle_Type" (Y axis, Vehicle_Type on category).
// [NAME CONFIRMED] [VERIFIED VIA DAX STUDIO — TMSCHEMA_MEASURES]
//
// Converts the raw kg-level emission figures aggregated in the fact table into
// metric tons, the standard unit for corporate carbon reporting.
MEASURE 'Fact_Shipments_with_ML'[Total CO2 Tons] =
    DIVIDE ( SUM ( Fact_Shipments_with_ML[CO2_Emission_kg] ), 1000 )


// Used on: "Executive Summary" — KPI card (reads 61,63K).
// [NAME CONFIRMED] [VERIFIED VIA DAX STUDIO — TMSCHEMA_MEASURES]
//
// Applies a $50/ton carbon price — a commonly cited mid-range figure used in
// voluntary carbon pricing and internal carbon fee models — to turn emissions
// into a projected financial liability.
//
// ⚠️ The multiplier is duplicated: it also lives in src/config.py as
//    CARBON_PRICE_PER_TON. Power BI cannot import from Python, so changing one
//    REQUIRES changing the other by hand. Nothing will warn you if you forget.
MEASURE 'Fact_Shipments_with_ML'[Carbon Tax Impact ($)] =
    [Total CO2 Tons] * 50


// Used on: "Executive Summary" — KPI card (reads 0,39).
// [NAME CONFIRMED] [VERIFIED VIA DAX STUDIO — TMSCHEMA_MEASURES]
//
// This one was previously a reconstruction from the measure name and the card
// value; the DAX Studio read confirmed it. Reads 0.3920 on the current dataset,
// which is the 0,39 on the card.
MEASURE 'Fact_Shipments_with_ML'[Average Delay Days] =
    AVERAGE ( Fact_Shipments_with_ML[Actual_Delay_Days] )


// Used on: "Early Warning Panel" — KPI card.
// [NAME CONFIRMED] [VERIFIED VIA DAX STUDIO — TMSCHEMA_MEASURES]
//
// The share of the current shipment population (respecting all active
// slicers/filters) that falls into the highest-risk delay tier — the project's
// primary early-warning KPI.
//
// The card is formatted as a percentage and reads %10,27; the donut on the same
// page independently shows 154 of 1,500 shipments in the High Risk tier
// (10,2%). 154/1500 = 10.27%, so the two agree. An earlier version
// of the data generator produced 84.3%; a panel where most shipments glow red
// carries no information, because an alert is only informative when it fires
// rarely. The thresholds behind the tiers are derived from a cost matrix, not
// percentiles — see src/config.py (COST_FN_OVER_FP, HIGH_RISK_THRESHOLD,
// MEDIUM_RISK_THRESHOLD).
MEASURE 'Fact_Shipments_with_ML'[High Risk Rate %] =
    DIVIDE (
        CALCULATE (
            COUNTROWS ( Fact_Shipments_with_ML ),
            Fact_Shipments_with_ML[Risk_Level] = "High Risk"
        ),
        COUNTROWS ( Fact_Shipments_with_ML )
    )


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SECTION 2 — Implicit aggregations in this .pbix (NOT measures)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Fields dragged straight onto a visual and aggregated automatically by Power
// BI. They carry no DAX definition and are listed so the inventory is complete.
//
//   "Executive Summary" / KPI card
//       Count of Shipment_ID  →  reads 1,5K
//       (documented as "Total Shipments" in docs/METHODOLOGY.md)
//
//   "Early Warning Panel" / donut chart
//       Count of Shipment_ID, grouped by Risk_Level
//       Reads 939 Low (62,6%) · 407 Medium (27,1%) · 154 High (10,2%)
//
//   "Early Warning Panel" / table (tableEx)
//       Shipment_ID · Weather_Condition (Dim_Route) · Risk_Level ·
//       Average of Delay_Risk_Probability · Vendor_ID
//       The probability column is an AVERAGE. Summing a probability would
//       produce a meaningless total; the visible column total is 0,22.
//
//   "Early Warning Panel" / Key Influencers
//       Target Risk_Level, explained by Vendor_Rating (Dim_Vendor),
//       Traffic_Density and Weather_Condition (Dim_Route). Uses no measure.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SECTION 3 — How the aggregation codes above were read
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// An implicit aggregation appears in the JSON as
//     {"Aggregation": {"Expression": {"Column": {"Property": "<col>"}},
//                      "Function": <int>}}
// Only two codes occur in this file, and both are confirmed against
// screenshots/ — this is not an assumed enum:
//
//     Function = 1  →  Average   (table header reads "Average of
//                                 Delay_Risk_Probability")
//     Function = 2  →  Count     (donut title reads "Count of Shipment_ID by
//                                 Risk_Level"; card labelled "Count of
//                                 Shipment_ID")
//
// `Function = 0` is believed to be Sum — the default for a numeric field — but
// no field in this file uses it, so it stays inferred. Do not assume any other
// mapping without re-calibrating against a rendered visual.
//
// ⚠️ IGNORE `queryRef` WHEN READING AGGREGATIONS. It is a human-readable label
//    captured when the field was first placed and it is NOT updated when the
//    user later changes the aggregation. Two fields in this file prove it:
//      · table probability column — queryRef "Sum(...Delay_Risk_Probability)"
//        but Function = 1, and the rendered header says Average
//      · Executive Summary count card — queryRef "Min(...Shipment_ID)"
//        but Function = 2, and the rendered card says Count of
//    `Function` is authoritative; `queryRef` is stale history.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RESOLVED — the "two different reports" problem
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A superseded .pbix (SHA-256 B4969442B8F8…, 132 KB) was committed here for a
// while. It did not match screenshots/: its donut summed Distance_km, its table
// summed the risk probability, it had no Vendor_ID column, and it bound a
// `High Risk Shipments` measure that appears in no documentation. The correct
// report was elsewhere on the author's machine; `High Risk Rate %` was added to
// it and it was saved over the repo copy.
//
// `High Risk Shipments` did NOT survive that replacement. It is bound to no
// visual, and TMSCHEMA_MEASURES did not return it — so it does not exist in the
// model either. It is gone for good; do not reintroduce it from an old document.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HOW TO RE-VERIFY THIS FILE AFTER CHANGING THE .PBIX
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// This file follows the .pbix, never the other way round. If you edit the report
// or the model, redo BOTH halves — they answer different questions:
//
// 1. EXPRESSIONS and the complete measure list — install DAX Studio (free),
//    connect it to the running Power BI Desktop instance and run
//        SELECT [Name], [Expression] FROM $SYSTEM.TMSCHEMA_MEASURES
//    One grid, every measure, full definitions. This is the only method that
//    sees a measure sitting on no visual, and the only one that reads the DAX.
//
// 2. BINDINGS — which visual on which page uses what — come from the report
//    definition JSON inside the .pbix ZIP, walked structurally. TMSCHEMA cannot
//    answer this; it knows the model, not the canvas.
//
// Add anything new, delete anything that disappeared, and mirror the change into
// README.md and docs/METHODOLOGY.md. A measure documented here that no longer
// exists is worse than an undocumented one.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
